# Loss contracts

The loss-contract family models policy and reinsurance terms as transformations of a loss sample. A [`LossTransform`](loss/LossTransform.md) maps each loss to what a contract pays on it, one output per input; a [`LossMeasure`](loss/LossMeasure.md) reduces a sample to a statistic. Composing the two gives the expected payment of a contract, which is the pure premium per claim.

## What This Family Owns

| You want | Use |
|---|---|
| The part of a loss above a deductible | [`deductible`](loss/deductible.md) |
| A loss capped at a policy limit | [`limit`](loss/limit.md) |
| A proportional share of the payments | [`coinsurance`](loss/coinsurance.md) |
| An excess-of-loss layer with a share | [`layer`](loss/layer.md) |
| The expected payment of a contract | [`mean`](loss/mean.md) applied to a transform's output |
| The types behind them | [`LossTransform`](loss/LossTransform.md), [`LossMeasure`](loss/LossMeasure.md) |

Build a contract once and apply it to any sample: the operators are vectorized and keep the platform of the data, and they are distribution-neutral, so simulated, historical and drawn losses use the same contract. Order matters when transforms are composed: apply them in the order the contract applies them.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1: one sample, four contracts

The payments of a deductible, a limit, a share and a layer on the same six losses.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const pluss::Series x = pluss::make_series({80.0, 150.0, 300.0, 700.0, 1200.0, 4000.0});
  out.writeln("loss        =", x.items<double>());
  out.writeln("deductible  =", actuarial::operators::deductible(100.0)(x).items<double>());
  out.writeln("limit       =", actuarial::operators::limit(500.0)(x).items<double>());
  out.writeln("coinsurance =", actuarial::operators::coinsurance(0.8)(x).items<double>());
  out.writeln("layer       =", actuarial::operators::layer(100.0, 500.0)(x).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 2: the expected payment of each contract

Applying the mean to each contract's payments.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const pluss::Series x = pluss::make_series({80.0, 150.0, 300.0, 700.0, 1200.0, 4000.0});
  const actuarial::LossMeasure mean = actuarial::operators::mean();
  out.writeln("ground up  =", mean(x).item<double>());
  out.writeln("deductible =", mean(actuarial::operators::deductible(100.0)(x)).item<double>());
  out.writeln("limit      =", mean(actuarial::operators::limit(500.0)(x)).item<double>());
  out.writeln("layer      =", mean(actuarial::operators::layer(100.0, 500.0)(x)).item<double>());
}
```

### Example 3: layers that split a loss

Three layers that meet end to end add back to the loss.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const pluss::Series x = pluss::make_series({80.0, 150.0, 300.0, 700.0, 1200.0, 4000.0});
  const pluss::Series total = actuarial::operators::limit(500.0)(x).add(actuarial::operators::layer(500.0, 1500.0)(x)).add(actuarial::operators::deductible(2000.0)(x));
  out.writeln(total.items<double>());
  out.writeln(x.items<double>());
}
```

